{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments ["<b>I will go down with you to Egypt, etc.</b> From the verse it appears that the Shekhinah went down with him, and it is difficult according to the words of the Sages, of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 15:5), who said that because Egypt was full of idolatry the Shekhinah was not there — and for this reason Moses needed to go outside the city to pray, as it is written (Exodus 9:29): ״כְּצֵאתִי אֶת הָעִיר״ (\"as I go out of the city\")." "<b>Or it may be</b> that the intent of the words of HaShem is along the lines of what He said (Psalms 91:15): ״עִמּוֹ אָנֹכִי בְצָרָה\" — that He hinted to him that, as it were, He bears the distress with him, and that is the meaning of His saying <b>I will go down with you to Egypt</b> — meaning, in the language of צַר (distress). And so we find that He revealed Himself to Moses in the thornbush to show him that He dwells in the thornbush, a place of thorns, on account of Israel's affliction. And in truth the Shekhinah never descended to Egypt, a defiled place. Except that I have seen that the Sages, of blessed memory, said in several places that the Shekhinah was with them in Egypt, as they said (Megillah 29a): ״גָּלוּ לְמִצְרַיִם שְׁכִינָה עִמָּהֶם\" (\"They were exiled to Egypt — the Shekhinah was with them\"), and likewise they said (Tanhuma, Vayehi): ״וַיִּשְׁתַּחוּ יִשְׂרָאֵל עַל רֹאשׁ הַמִּטָּה״ (\"Israel bowed upon the head of the bed\") — to the Shekhinah that was there, etc. And they further said (Genesis Rabbah 94:9) that the Shekhinah completed the count of seventy souls who went down to Egypt, for when you count them individually they are only sixty-nine (ס״ט) — behold, the Shekhinah went down to Egypt." "<b>Indeed,</b> I will enlighten you: the gradations of the light of the Shekhinah are many. For you will find that regarding ten who sit and engage in Torah (Avot 3:6), our Sages taught that the Shekhinah dwells among them, and even two and one, the Sages of blessed memory said, the Shekhinah is present. Yet we find that the Shekhinah did not descend among Israel until after they made the Mishkan, and after several preparations that are mentioned — and in its descent they saw the deed of YHWH, and the glory of YHWH filled the Mishkan (Exodus 40:35), something that is not experienced thusly, nor anything like it, when engaging in Torah even a thousandfold. Rather, certainly there are gradations beyond count for the indwelling of the Shekhinah, in the sod of ״כִּי גָבֹהַּ מֵעַל גָּבֹהַּ שֹׁמֵר״ (Ecclesiastes 5:7), and the light increases in its indwelling according to the level of the place that hosts the indwelling of the Shekhinah. Go forth and learn: the indwelling of the Shekhinah at Mount Sinai, and below that its indwelling in the Beit HaMikdash, and below that the indwelling of the Shekhinah upon the prophet and in the synagogue and the beit midrash and among ten who engage in Torah, and below that in the gradations of the minyan. And with this the words of the Sages of blessed memory are reconciled: the Shekhinah that did not descend — that is the aspect of the highest light, which is the one that inspires prophecy, and therefore Moses said ״כְּצֵאתִי אֶת הָעִיר״; but the aspect of the light found among those who study Torah and the like did go down with Jacob our father, and was there until the day Israel went out from Egypt." "And although in His light, may He be blessed, it is not justified to make a distinction — this is true, for in His divinity, may He be blessed, He is uniform light; but in the light that shines forth from Him, it is to that light that gradations are ascribed. And this is also found in the aspects of the souls, which are light from the light of the throne of His glory, may He be blessed — among them there are wondrous distinctions. And understand this." "And with this we also find satisfaction in what the verse says, <b>and I will bring you up</b> — that He repeated to say a second time ״אָנֹכִי\" and did not suffice with what He wrote at first, ״אָנֹכִי אֵרֵד\" etc. — that He intended to speak of the supernal aspect: ״I will bring you up\" etc. And this is the sod of what He said (Exodus 20:2): ״אָנֹכִי ה׳ אֱלֹהֶיךָ\" — and it is the aspect of the supernal Shekhinah, the year of the jubilee, the year of forty-nine (מ״ט), and it is the sod of the forty-nine times that the exodus from Egypt is mentioned in the Torah." "<b>Further,</b> it intends by saying <b>and I</b> a second time, to hint to him that the salvation would come through Him, may He be blessed. This is along the lines of what they said (Mekhilta Bo): ״וַיּוֹצִיאֵנוּ ה׳״ and so forth — not by means of an angel and not [by a messenger] and so forth, but rather the Holy One, blessed be He, Himself in His glory and so forth. And this is the meaning of His saying ״וְאָנֹכִי״ — in My glory I will bring you up, and so forth (Deuteronomy 6:21, Exodus 13:16)." "<b>And the reason</b> for the doubling <b>I will bring you up, and also bring you up</b> is to hint to him about two ascents: the first is the exodus from Egypt, and the second is that He would bring him up from there so that he not be buried in the land of Egypt — which is the fear that he feared, as we have written (Genesis 46:3). And He said the word ״גַּם״ to hint at an added multiplicity of what they would bring up from Egypt, both in the aspect of spiritual ascent and in the aspect of the refinements that they would refine there — the sparks of holiness without end:" "<b>Or</b> it may intend <b>and also bring you up</b> to hint to the spiritual portion that descends with him to Egypt. And see the Zohar (Exodus 4) in the explanation of the verse ״יָצְאוּ כָל צִבְאוֹת ה׳״ (Exodus 12:41)." "<b>and Joseph shall put his hand</b> and so forth. It intends to promise him that he would not die in his [Joseph's] lifetime, also because several times Jacob, whose memory is a blessing, mentioned death regarding Joseph and said also (Genesis 37:35) ״כִּי אֵרֵד אֶל בְּנִי״ and so forth. Therefore He returned to promise him that he would not suffer the unintentional error that comes (Ecclesiastes 10:5) and so forth." "<b>Further,</b> it intends to tell him that Jacob's ascent would not take place during his lifetime but only after death, and that is the meaning of <b>and Joseph</b> and so forth. From this too he knew that he had no permission to leave Egypt during his lifetime, so that the word of HaShem would be fulfilled — that Joseph would put his hand on his eyes. And if he were to leave, the matter could not be fulfilled."], :book :genesis, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 46, :notes {5 "The Mekhilta citation is from the portion of Bo. The verse references in parentheses (Deuteronomy 6:21, Exodus 13:16) appear to point to other occurrences of ואנכי or related language rather than to the Mekhilta source.", 7 "The Zohar reference '(שמות ד׳)' likely means the portion of Shemot, chapter/volume 4 of the Zohar rather than Exodus chapter 4."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T19:49:33.132977233Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}